For the filtration audit, use the finite translation-complete test object

```text
K_L = the oriented 2-skeleton of (Z/LZ)^4,    L>=3,
```

with one Hermitian line at each vertex and arbitrary periodic unitary link
transports. `K_L` is a finite two-dimensional CW complex with four edge
directions and every elementary plaquette; the higher-dimensional cells of
the four-torus are not included. Let

```text
U_mu(x):L_x->L_(x+mu)
```

be its sealed periodic edge transport. On
`H_0(K_L)=direct-sum_x L_x`, define the global unitary shifts

```text
(T_mu psi)_x = U_mu(x)^dagger psi_(x+mu),
nabla_mu = T_mu - I
```

with periodic indices. They are everywhere-defined unitary endomorphisms with
globally defined inverses. On a nonperiodic finite complex the same formula
may be compressed to interior cells, but no free-group representation is
claimed for that compression. Because `C_1` stores an edge coefficient in
its source fiber, the identity-metric adjoint supplies the directional
vertex-to-edge difference without another identification. For the candidate
`a=b=1`,

```text
(D^sharp psi)_(e_mu(x)) = (nabla_mu psi)_x.
```

This equality is an exact consequence of the source-fiber convention, not a
separately normalized derivative.